\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Classical inverse knobs for several target response laws. Since \(R_{\rm  cl}(\tau )=\tau /\pi \), the transcribed classical knob is simply \(T_{{\rm  cl}\leftarrow B}(\theta )/\pi =R_B(\theta )\). The curves show how a linear classical response must be read on a generally non-symmetry-preserving scale in order to mimic a quantum, softmax, or Heaviside response as a function of the displayed angle.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:inverse-knobs}{{1}{4}{Classical inverse knobs for several target response laws. Since \(R_{\rm cl}(\tau )=\tau /\pi \), the transcribed classical knob is simply \(T_{{\rm cl}\leftarrow B}(\theta )/\pi =R_B(\theta )\). The curves show how a linear classical response must be read on a generally non-symmetry-preserving scale in order to mimic a quantum, softmax, or Heaviside response as a function of the displayed angle}{figure.1}{}}
